            <div xml:id="d211">

                <pb facs="0299" n="195" xml:id="pg195"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/3931: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-02-03T16:00:00+08:00">February 3, 1932—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 8:58 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>189. Legation’s 186, February 3, 1 p.m.<anchor n="38" xml:id="d211-f1"/>
                    Following from Harbin: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“February 3, 10 a.m. 1. Situation at and near Harbin unchanged.</p>
                        <p>2. Yesterday Japanese military demanded from the railway board 10 trains
                            to be used in transporting Japanese troops from Anganghsi to Harbin.
                            Soviet side agreed but Chinese side refused despite open threats of
                            Japanese military. Traffic on western line has been stopped presumably
                            by Chinese military, leaving only eastern line open.</p>
                        <p>3. General Li Tu is now commander in chief of all organized Kirin
                            troops.</p>
                        <p>4. Ohashi states that he has received a telegram from Honjo regretting
                            that Japanese Army has been so slow in reaching Harbin.”</p></quote></p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >Perkins</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
